WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.532
Likert-style responses illustrate ordinal data well.

00:00:03.532 --> 00:00:08.487
A five is higher than a four, but the psychological distance from one to two may not equal

00:00:08.487 --> 00:00:11.347
the distance from four to five.

00:00:11.347 --> 00:00:16.672
Means are commonly reported in practice, particularly for composite scales.

00:00:16.672 --> 00:00:20.637
But for a single coarse item, medians, distributions,

00:00:20.637 --> 00:00:24.977
and rank-based summaries may better respect the measurement assumptions.

00:00:24.977 --> 00:00:30.997
The important point is to recognize the assumption rather than use the mean automatically.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:37.682
Connect this idea to an e-commerce team preparing customer, purchase, and web-event data.

00:00:37.682 --> 00:00:43.362
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:43.362 --> 00:00:51.197
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:51.197 --> 00:00:57.172
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:57.172 --> 00:01:02.880
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
